case we started off  with theta as 0 and then we increment  theta by 0.1 on every single animation  loop then we add a second ball but in  this case instead of starting at theta  as zero  we started at 0.4 and then we add a  third one that started at 0.8 and a  fourth one and a full row of them and  then the second row and another layer  and in each time we simply offset the  starting point that allows us to create  this really beautiful wave-like pattern  and by layering a lot of them we get  this really interesting interesting  motion or they go out of sync and they  keep moving around and then at some  point they come together and they  synchronize for just a moment and then  they go apart go apart again so that's  what that's one way to generate those  offset patterns the other way to  generate those offset patterns is to use  different amplitudes so instead of  oscillating all of them at the same  amplitude so oscillating them all at 300  pixels or something you can choose a  different value for each one of them and  that will give a different kind of  effect and that that's what's happening  here so instead of oscillating each dot  at the same time the horizontal motion  there's a different amplitude for  depending on which row that dot belongs  in oh let's go let's go back to that  rocket example once more so in this  example you'll notice that the rocket is  moving on its own but it's also react  I can actually drag the rocket with my  mouse cursor rotated men manually or  even just like toss it and there's a  little bit of momentum to it so how do  you do that how does it know that when  your mouse cursor is moving around the  page where that rocket needs to be what  angle is it going to be at in that  rotation so in order to do that we need  to talk about yet another mathematical  concept and this one's known as solving  triangles so solving triangles is this  idea where you have a triangle and you  know something about it you know one  angle you know maybe you know a couple  of things or you know one side or a  couple of sides or a mixture of like one  angle in one side and you're trying to  find all the other missing sides or the  missing angles so for now I'm going to  focus on a simplified case of this which  I tend to encounter a lot while making  animations and that's the right angle  triangle so since it's a right angle  triangle it has a few properties one  angle we know is 90 degrees and then  there are a couple of other angles which  we know have to be less than 90 degrees  and they should add up to 90 degrees and  then it has three sides the opposite the  adjacent adjacent which is the base of  the triangle and then the hypotenuse so  once we know this we can start talking  about how would we go about solving this  triangle so we often find ourselves in  these scenarios where okay I know this  is a right angle triangle I maybe know  two of the sides and I need to figure  out what the angle is for the between  the hypotenuse and the base or one of  the other angles or I might know that  angle and one of the sides and I need to  figure out the third side so how do we  do that well for that we need to go back  to high school for a bit you might  remember this mnemonic device of silca  Toa well that's exactly what we're going  to use here to to figure out those  angles or the missing information so we  have these three formulas which you tend  to use quite a lot to solve triangles so  sine of theta is over H cos of theta is  over H and tan of theta is over a so if  I wanted to figure out what that angle  theta is I can use that last formula of  ten of theta is over a and calculate  theta as our tangent of over a so our  tangent is simply in inverse of 10 so if  you move 10 from the left hand side of  the equation to the right hand side it  turns into our tangent so let's see this  in action this is a demo from the p5.js  examples and it's a little example of  eye tracking or sort of a simplified  version of eye tracking so you'll notice  that these three eyes sort of track the  mouse cursor which is the purple dot and  they try to always look at that mouse  cursor if you look at a little bit  closer you'll notice that the pupil of  the eye the the black part is simply  rotating about the center of the eye so  it's a very similar setup to that rocket  example except instead of rotating  outside the moon is rotating inside the  eye so as I move the cursor around the  page what I need to do is figure out the  angle of rotation for that particular  circle and the way I do that is by  solving triangles so you might be asking  wait what triangle what are we solving  here so let me enable some debugging  here so you'll notice that if we connect  the purple dot which is the mouse cursor  and the blue dot which is the center of  the eye it actually forms a right hand  right right angled triangle and since we  know the location of the center of the  eye and we know the location of the  mouse cursor we can actually we actually  know the the base of the triangle and we  know the height of the triangle so we  can use those two values and put it into  our arctangent formula and calculate  that angle that the eye needs to be so  in this demo what's happening is we're  solving three triangles at the same time  one for one for each eye and as the  mouse cursor moves around the page it's  simply figuring out the angle and  rotating the black circle by that value  and this is sort of a more elaborate  version of that eye-tracking this one  was built by Hakeem al khattab for the  net 404 just therefore for error page  and along with that are tracking idea it  has a few more things happening here so  the eyes are blinking and you can add  more layers of animation to that initial  reactivity that you're adding that  you're creating let's look at another  example of solving triangles so in this  case this is a little animation I made a  few years ago where I'm trying to  replicate a very cartoony lighting  system where you can see the the yellow  circle acts as the source of light and  based on how close or far away I'm from  the triangle that that controls this  glossy disc lossy shine that you can see  on the page there so again how am I  using solving problem solving triangles  here let's go to the debug version of  this so in this case you'll notice that  we know the location of the center of  the triangle we know the location of the  source of light and what we really need  to figure out is the two points that  make up that glass and there's one other  variable that we actually know in this  case we know the spread of the light and  spread of light in this case is a  triangle that we're using here and in  this case that spread of light is  controlled by the distance between the  the source of the light and the center  of the triangle so the further away I am  from the triangle the wider that  triangle gets and the closer I go to it  the narrower that triangle gets so in  this case I actually know the angle of  that triangle at all times and and then  finally we also know the distance from  the center of the triangle to where that  glass is being drawn that's the the take  green line that you see there so since  we know that one distance we know the  angle we can actually calculate the  location where those Wordie seas are  going to be placed and that allows us to  build figure out how long that glass  needs to be and render it out as a as a  stroke  one more example of solving triangles so  this one is from the paper J's  documentation site they have an example  of metal balls normally metal balls are  built using using shaders and this is a  slightly different version of metal  balls where instead of using shaders  it's built using canvas and just  connecting shapes together so in this  case what you'll notice is the meta  balls are made up of two circles the the  little circle and then the big circle  that I can move around with my mouse  cursor and then if the two circles come  close enough there's a connector that  come that becomes between the two of  them and that's where we're using the  idea of solving triangles so for this  particular one I have debugging enabled  so you'll notice that as I move the  circle close enough the connector shows  up and then the connector is connecting  two points on each of the circles and  those two points have handles that  decide how narrow or wide that connector  needs to be so in this scenario what we  need to do is figure out that angle of  that that connector that's Ari they have  that handle and the way we can do that  is we know the radius of the circles we  know the point at which that that handle  is going to be generated so again it  form forms a triangle and we can use  that to calculate the angle of that  handle and then generate this connector  so as I go closer or further away you  can see the connector moving wide or  coming closer and of course this concept  is used a lot in in sort of physics  applications so for game development so  here I have a link to a blog post by  Rachel Smith which covers the idea of  using solving triangles to to actually  build out leavers so sort of similar  idea to what certain games like Angry  Birds or you know any of any of those  kinds of games tend to use so if you if  kind of interested in this idea I would  recommend highly recommend reading that  blog post to get a lot more detail about  how to solve triangles and I'll share a  link to that in my slides after the talk  and finally I wanted to talk a little  bit about easing so if you've ever done  CSS animations you're probably dealt  with some kind of easing right when we  create our animation we specify what  kind of easing we want but it's linear  or a sine wave or cubic or quadratic all  kinds of stuff right so CSS has a lot of  easing predefined and we can simply  select which one we want to apply and  each one of those easing that we choose  is known as an easing function so an  easing function is something that drives  the relationship between time and the  progress of that animation and a lot of  those functions are predefined except a  cubic Bezier one that's something that  we can actually configure to our liking  even in CSS so a cubic Bezier easing is  essentially a curve of this sort and  usually you need four four variables to  generate that curve one the start value  the end value and then the location of  the two handles however in CSS that  start value is always set to be zero  zero the end value is always set to be  one one so the only thing we need to  provide to generate this curve is the  location of the two handles and that's  what those four numbers are so if you've  ever written a CSS animation with cubic  material all it by four numbers those  are the locations of those two handles  so the first one is the x coordinate of  the first handle second one is the y  coordinate of the first handle and then  the last two are the X and the y  coordinate of the second handle so we  can use this to generate more customized  curves that define the easing that we  want to use and there's many tools out  there if you just search for cubic  Bezier you'll find hundreds of tools out  there that allow you to generate those  curves but when you're working with  JavaScript animation libraries you can  go one step further you can actually  build your own easing function and one  of the most popular ways to do this is  on the web especially is to use  something called Penner's equations in  Penner's equations are a certain type of  equations  take four variables and then construct  how how your animation is going to  progress so how is it going to go from  the starting point to the end point and  the four variables that you need that  that equation receives is starting with  the current time  so as you're tweeting from start to end  what is the current time in your  animation loop and this can be in any  unit as you as you want it could be in  seconds in milliseconds in frame counts  as long as you're using the same unit  throughout your throughout your app  throughout your animation  the second argument that's passed in is  the start value so this is the start  value of the property that you're  animating the third is Delta which is  the change between the start value and  the end value of the enemy of the  property that you're animating and then  finally the duration the total duration  of the animation or the tween or the  transition that you're generating and  then the body of the function usually  consists of something like this where  you have the start value plus the delta  times some kind of multiplier and that  multiplier usually comes from a  transform so that transform kind of  decides how your animation is going to  behave whether it's going to have a  linear ease if it's going to have a  bounce ease or some kind of really crazy  East that you're creating so let's look  at an example of an easing that's  generated using the Penner's equations  so this is something I found from  Potocki who's a jiff I really talented  Jeff animator and on his blog he shared  this function that he uses quite often  for to generate easing so down here  you'll notice is the the actual pen or  equation and that start value plus some  kind of multiplier that we're generating  and then the multiplied by Delta so that  transform function is really the only  one we mostly need to care about so in  this case this is the transform function  that's being defined so there's a lot of  math going on here and it takes a while  to get your head around that and  understand exactly what's happening here  but the nice thing about here about this  is you don't need to worry about it you  can just go ahead and  start using it because the only thing  that you need to care about is that  first line of code the G equals six by  modifying that value of G you can  generate all kinds of and all kinds of  easing so it's a really nice formula  that allows you to generate different  kinds of easing x' just by modifying  that one value here's another example of  bounce easing so this is something from  mat deloria's eases library so generally  if you go and look at code for a bounce  transition you'll notice that there's a  lot of code there there's like four or  five lines of code that decide what kind  of that animation what that function  needs to be to generate that easing but  this is a much more stripped-down  simplified version of that so in this  case the nice thing is you'll notice  this number here at 13 that constant is  the only thing that drives the  springiness of this bounce easing so but  by increasing or decreasing that value  you can control whether this is going to  be a really really stiff straight spring  so you know it bounces very slightly and  stops right away or it's going to be a  really really flexible spring where it  wobbles around many times before coming  to a stop so again it's really you don't  need to understand the full math here  but just by knowing what one number you  can change in order to generate  different eases allows you to construct  really interesting behaviors and once  you understand this idea of what a  Penner equation is and what that  transform part is you can start using it  to construct your own custom easing  functions  so most JavaScript animation libraries  will allow you to define that so you  don't need to worry about constructing  the full pen or equation the only part  you need to worry about creating is the  actual transform itself so the one that  depends on the current time so this is  an example of how you would define a  custom easing for anime Jas which is a  fairly popular JavaScript animation  library so you can replace this with the  body of this function with any of the  previous two examples that I showed and  and use those custom easing patterns in  your own when you're working with  animators and that allows you to make  interesting things  like this demo that I created were on  every click all of those little layers  of the Box animate with a different  spring level so in this case I'm  constructing five or different five or  six different kinds of spring easing and  then layering them on top of each other  to create this staggered effect where  each box sort of bounces at a different  rate and that's about it  thank you for coming out
 

